---

<BaseLayout
  title="Dropdown · Bootstrap HTML"
  description="Native HTML dropdowns using the Popover API and CSS Anchor Positioning, styled with Bootstrap 5.3.8."
>
  <h1>Dropdown</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Toggle contextual overlays with the Popover API
    (<code>popover=&quot;auto&quot;</code>) and CSS Anchor Positioning. The
    browser handles stacking, light-dismiss, and placement — no Dropdown
    plugin and no Popper.
  </p>

  <DocsMeta id="dropdown" at="start" />

  <h2 class="h4">How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>command=&quot;toggle-popover&quot;</code> and
      <code>commandfor</code> replace
      <code>data-bs-toggle=&quot;dropdown&quot;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The menu is <code>.dropdown-menu</code> with
      <code>popover=&quot;auto&quot;</code>. The browser puts it in the top
      layer and closes it on outside click or Escape.
    </li>
    <li>
      CSS Anchor Positioning tethers the menu to
      <code>.dropdown-toggle</code> (via <code>anchor-name</code> /
      <code>position-anchor</code> and <code>anchor-scope</code> on the
      wrapper). <code>.dropup</code>, <code>.dropend</code>,
      <code>.dropstart</code>, <code>.dropdown-center</code>, and
      <code>.dropdown-menu-end</code> still choose the side.
      <code>position-try-fallbacks</code> flips when there isn’t room.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>:popover-open</code> replaces <code>.show</code> for the open
      state.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>command</code> works on <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, not
      <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sibling <code>popover=&quot;auto&quot;</code> menus still replace
      each other. A nested menu must sit inside the open parent
      popover (or its invoker must) so the ancestor stays open.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Example
    title="Single button"
    lead="Any single .btn can be turned into a dropdown toggle. command/commandfor opens a popover menu."
    code={examples.dropdown}
  />

  <Example
    title="Dropdown link"
    lead="command only works on buttons, so this is a button styled as a .btn rather than an &lt;a href&gt; trigger."
    code={linkButtonCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
      Bootstrap’s plugin also accepts <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> toggles.
      Invoker Commands require a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Button variants"
    lead="The same markup works with any button variant."
    code={variantsCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Split button"
    lead="Add .dropdown-toggle-split on the caret button for proper spacing. The first button is a separate action; only the caret toggles the menu."
    code={splitCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Sizing"
    lead="Button dropdowns work with .btn-lg and .btn-sm, including split buttons."
    code={sizingCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Dark dropdowns"
    lead="Opt into darker dropdowns by adding .dropdown-menu-dark onto an existing .dropdown-menu."
    code={darkCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
      Dark variants for components were deprecated in v5.3.0 with color
      modes. Prefer <code>data-bs-theme=&quot;dark&quot;</code> on the
      component or a parent instead of <code>.dropdown-menu-dark</code>.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Navbar"
    lead="A dark dropdown inside a navbar. Placement still uses CSS Anchor Positioning — Popper is not involved, including in navbars."
    code={navbarDarkCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Centered"
    lead="Make the dropdown menu centered below the toggle with .dropdown-center on the parent."
    code={centeredCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Dropup"
    lead="Trigger dropdown menus above elements by adding .dropup to the parent."
    code={dropupCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Dropup centered"
    lead="Make the dropup menu centered above the toggle with .dropup-center and .dropup on the parent."
    code={dropupCenterCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Dropend"
    lead="Trigger dropdown menus at the right of the elements by adding .dropend to the parent."
    code={dropendCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Dropstart"
    lead="Trigger dropdown menus at the left of the elements by adding .dropstart to the parent. Directions are flipped in RTL."
    code={dropstartCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Submenu"
    lead="Put .dropdown-submenu on the list item and nest another popover menu inside the parent menu. Bootstrap 5 dropped this because Popper could not keep the ancestor open. Nested popover=&quot;auto&quot; can. Add .dropstart on the submenu to open the other way. position-try-fallbacks flips when there is no room."
    code={submenuCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
      The nested <code>&lt;ul popover=&quot;auto&quot;&gt;</code> has to be a
      descendant of the open parent popover. A sibling menu would close
      the parent. The toggle is a <code>&lt;button class=&quot;dropdown-item dropdown-toggle&quot;&gt;</code>,
      not an <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code>.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Menu items"
    lead="You can use &lt;a&gt; or &lt;button&gt; elements as dropdown items."
    code={menuItemsCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Text items"
    lead="Create non-interactive dropdown items with .dropdown-item-text."
    code={itemTextCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Active"
    lead="Add .active to items in the dropdown to style them as active. Use aria-current for assistive technologies."
    code={activeCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Disabled"
    lead="Add .disabled to items in the dropdown to style them as disabled."
    code={disabledCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Menu alignment"
    lead="Add .dropdown-menu-end to right-align the menu with the toggle. Directions are mirrored in RTL."
    code={alignmentEndCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Responsive alignment"
    lead="Use .dropdown-menu{-sm|-md|-lg|-xl|-xxl}-end to right-align from a breakpoint up, or combine .dropdown-menu-end with .dropdown-menu-*-start to switch back."
    code={alignmentResponsiveCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
      Bootstrap required <code>data-bs-display=&quot;static&quot;</code> so
      Popper would not override these classes. CSS Anchor Positioning
      honors the alignment classes directly, so that attribute is not
      needed.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Headers"
    lead="Add a header to label sections of actions in any dropdown menu."
    code={headersCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Dividers"
    lead="Separate groups of related menu items with a divider."
    code={dividersCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Text"
    lead="Place any freeform text within a dropdown menu with text and use spacing utilities."
    code={textCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Forms"
    lead="Put a form within a dropdown menu, or make it into a dropdown menu, and use margin or padding utilities."
    code={formsCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Dropdown options"
    lead="data-bs-offset and data-bs-reference are Popper options and have no native equivalent. The menus still open as popovers."
    code={offsetCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
      <code>data-bs-offset</code> and
      <code>data-bs-reference=&quot;parent&quot;</code> require Popper.
      Native menus always anchor to <code>.dropdown-toggle</code>. Extra
      offset can be approximated with margin on the menu if needed.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Auto close behavior"
    lead="popover=&quot;auto&quot; light-dismisses on outside click (and Escape). popover=&quot;manual&quot; stays open until the toggle (or hide-popover) runs. Item clicks only close the menu when those items use command=&quot;hide-popover&quot;."
    code={autoCloseCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
      Bootstrap’s <code>autoClose</code> option is JavaScript. Closest
      native mapping: default and “clickable outside” use
      <code>popover=&quot;auto&quot;</code>; “clickable inside” and
      “manual close” use <code>popover=&quot;manual&quot;</code>. Default
      and inside also put <code>command=&quot;hide-popover&quot;</code> on
      items so a click inside can close the menu. Clicking another
      <code>auto</code> popover will not dismiss a
      <code>manual</code> one.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <DocsMeta id="dropdown" at="end" />

  <h2 class="h4" id="focus-group">Arrow keys</h2>
  <p>
    Tab still moves through
    <code>.dropdown-item</code> when no extra script is loaded. Arrow
    keys, Home, and End are optional. Wrap the whole
    <code>.dropdown</code> in
    <code>&lt;pe-focus-group&gt;</code> and load
    <code>src/behaviors/focus-group.js</code>. Without that module the
    element is only a wrapper: the menu still opens, and Tab still
    reaches every item.
  </p>
  <p>
    Up and Left focus the previous item. Down and Right focus the next.
    Home and End jump to the ends. From the toggle, the same keys open
    the popover and then move. Disabled items are skipped.
    <code>items</code> is the selector for what can be focused.
    In a navbar the wrapper goes inside the
    <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>, because a list may only contain list items.
    A closed submenu is skipped; once it is open its items are in the
    same group.
  </p>

  <Example
    title="Focus group"
    lead="pe-focus-group adds arrow keys, Home, and End. The disabled item is skipped. Escape still closes the popover."
    code={focusGroupCode}
  />
  <script>
    import "../../behaviors/focus-group.js";
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
